---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { challenges, categories, totalPoints } from '../lib/challenges';

const cats = categories();
const commandCount = 50;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What Learmux is, how it is built (Astro + TypeScript + MDX + JSON), and how to run and deploy it."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>About Learmux</h1>
    <p>
      Learmux is a small, self-contained learning platform for <strong>Termux</strong> — the terminal
      emulator and Linux environment for Android — and for the Linux command line more broadly. It pairs
      short written tutorials with an interactive capture-the-flag terminal, so you can read about
      <code>grep</code> and then immediately use it to pull a flag out of a noisy log.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>Every layer was chosen to keep the project explainable and cheap to run:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Astro</strong> renders the site statically: pages, tutorials, navigation, SEO and the sitemap. No server to maintain.</li>
      <li><strong>TypeScript</strong> powers the CTF engine — an explicit command parser, a virtual filesystem, and a deterministic shell that tracks flags, hints and progress.</li>
      <li><strong>Custom CSS</strong> (no framework) gives the mobile-first interface and the terminal its own look, including on-screen-keyboard handling.</li>
      <li><strong>Markdown / MDX</strong> holds the tutorials, so lessons are just content files.</li>
      <li><strong>JSON</strong> holds the {challenges.length} challenge definitions — filesystems, flags and hints — with no database required for the prototype.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>By the numbers</h2>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{challenges.length}</span><span class="l">challenges</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{totalPoints}</span><span class="l">points</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{cats.length}</span><span class="l">categories</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{commandCount}+</span><span class="l">shell commands</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2>How the sandbox works</h2>
    <p>
      There is no real operating system behind the terminal. A challenge seeds a virtual filesystem, and
      your keystrokes are parsed by an explicit tokeniser and pipeline executor. That means the state is
      completely predictable: the same command always produces the same result, and resetting a challenge
      simply restores the seed. Commands that would touch a network or the device — <code>nmap</code>,
      <code>curl</code>, <code>pkg</code>, <code>ps</code> — return authored output defined per challenge,
      so nothing depends on the host.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      Note: this is a learning prototype. Flags live in the client-side JSON, so a determined player could
      read them ahead of time. That is fine for teaching — the next step would be moving challenge
      definitions and flag checks behind an API.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Roadmap</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Accounts and a server-side leaderboard (swap the JSON store for a database).</li>
      <li>Authoring UI so non-developers can add challenges.</li>
      <li>More categories: scripting, process inspection, file forensics, package management.</li>
      <li>Optional real sandboxing (WASM shell) for advanced challenges.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Run it locally</h2>
    <pre><code>npm install
npm run dev      # http://localhost:4321
npm run build    # static output in dist/
npm run preview  # preview the production build</code></pre>
    <p>
      To regenerate the challenge JSON from the authoring script:
      <code>node scripts/generate-challenges.mjs</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Deploy</h2>
    <p>
      The build output is fully static, which makes Cloudflare Pages a natural host: connect the
      repository, set the build command to <code>npm run build</code> and the output directory to
      <code>dist</code>. Any static host works the same way.
    </p>

    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/challenges/">Start a challenge →</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/learn/">Browse tutorials</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
